course: english
lesson: 38
title: Standard English and dialect: "we was" in talk, "we were" in writing
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A dialect is the way a group of people speak, with its own words and its own grammar; Kai from Leeds says "We was going to the shop", his gran says "Put t'kettle on, love", and both follow rules their whole town shares.
explain: Standard English is the dialect used for school writing, exams, newspapers and formal letters all over the English-speaking world; it is not "better", it is the shared one, like a common plug that fits every socket.
explain: Accent is different: it is how words sound; you can speak Standard English in a Glasgow, Lagos or Texas accent, and you can speak dialect in a posh one.
explain: So when a teacher writes "we were" over "we was", she is not saying Kai's family talks wrong; she is handing him a second tool, the way a footballer has home kit and away kit and wears each at the right match.
explain: Switching between home talk and Standard English depending on who you are with is called code-switching, and most people do it every day without noticing: one voice for mates, another for the head teacher.
fact: A dialect is a variety of a language with its own words and grammar, spoken by a group or in a place.
fact: Standard English is the variety used in formal writing and formal speech; it is one dialect among many, and it is the one schools teach for writing.
fact: An accent is the way words sound; any dialect, including Standard English, can be spoken in any accent.
fact: "We was", "I done it", "them books", "I seen it" and "I ain't got none" are common in many dialects; the Standard English forms are "we were", "I did it", "those books", "I saw it" and "I haven't got any".
fact: Two negatives together, as in "I didn't see nobody", are a regular pattern in many dialects (and in languages such as French); Standard English says "I didn't see anybody".
fact: Dialects follow rules: speakers of "we was" use "was" with every person, which is a regular pattern, not a random mistake.
fact: In parts of Yorkshire and Lancashire "the" is shortened to t' ("Put t'kettle on").
fact: "Youse" (Ireland, Scotland, Liverpool) and "y'all" (the American South) are plural forms of "you"; Standard English uses "you" for one or many.
fact: In Multicultural London English "man" can mean "I", as in "Man's going shop."
fact: In Bristol "Where's it to?" means "Where is it?"; in Singapore English "Can or not?" means "Is that possible?" or "Is that okay?"
fact: Code-switching means changing how you speak or write depending on who you are with and why.
fact: SATs grammar questions in England ask for Standard English, so pupils need to know the standard forms as well as their home forms.
fact: Laughing at someone's dialect is unkind and also wrong about language: every variety of English has grammar.
exercise: Kai from Leeds writes "We was going to the shop." His teacher writes "we were". Kai says "But that's how we talk." What is the fairest reply? | Your way is real dialect; school writing uses "we were" | Your family speaks wrong English and should stop | Both forms are equally fine to use in a SATs test | "We was" is just a spelling mistake you must fix
exercise: Gran in Yorkshire says "Put t'kettle on, love." Ruby asks why she drops "the". What is the answer? | In her dialect "the" is shortened to t' | Gran has forgotten how to say the word "the" | It is a mistake that comes from being in a hurry | It is an old word borrowed from French
exercise: Teacher: "In writing, 'I done it' becomes..." | "I did it" | "I doned it" | "I have did it" | "I does it"
exercise: Lewis from London says "Man's going shop, innit." What does "man" mean here? | I | My dad | Any man | A shop worker
exercise: How would Lewis write "Man's going shop" in a formal letter? | I'm going to the shop. | Man is going to shop. | The man is going shopping, innit. | Mans going to the shops.
exercise: Grandad in Belfast says "Youse are late." What is the Standard English? | You are late. | You is late. | Yous are late. | Yourselves are late.
exercise: Why do "youse" and "y'all" exist? | To show "you" means more than one person | Because those speakers never learned "you" | To be rude to strangers | Because "you" is a newer word
exercise: An American cousin says "Y'all coming?" A British child says "That's not English." What is true? | It is American Southern English, a real kind of English | Only British English counts as real English | Y'all is a word that came from Spanish | It is only ever used in cowboy films
exercise: Which is the Standard English for "them books" in writing? | those books | them there books | these here books | that books
exercise: Mum in Bristol asks "Where's it to?" What does she mean? | Where is it? | Where is it going? | Who is it for? | When is it due?
exercise: Year 6 SATs: tick the sentence in Standard English. | We were late. | We was late. | We been late. | We is late.
exercise: Aanya in Singapore says "Can or not?" What would she write in Standard English? | Is that okay? | Can it or not can? | Is not can? | Can not or can?
exercise: What is code-switching? | Changing how you talk for different people and situations | A code for opening a locked phone | Swapping slang words for numbers | Speaking two languages at once in every sentence
exercise: What is the difference between accent and dialect? | Accent is sound; dialect is words and grammar | They are just two names for one thing | Accent is for the North and dialect for the South | Dialect is spoken and accent is written
exercise: Which is the Standard English of "I ain't got none"? | I haven't got any. | I ain't got any. | I haven't got none. | I don't got none.
exercise: Why is "we was" a pattern and not just a random slip? | Speakers use "was" with every person, like a rule | It comes from children mishearing their teachers | It is used only when people are very tired | It is a short way of writing "we always said"
test: Your mate laughs at the new kid's Glasgow accent and says "he can't even talk proper." What is wrong with that? | It's unkind, and every accent and dialect has rules | Nothing, because Glasgow speech is not really English at all | He is right, accents are mistakes | Only the new kid is allowed to laugh
test: ★★★ Which sentence is Standard English but could be spoken in any accent? | We were going to the beach. | We was going t'beach. | Us were going to the beach. | We were going to't beach.
test: In a cartoon, the posh character always speaks "properly" and the farmer always "wrongly". What is the cartoon getting wrong? | The farmer's dialect has rules too | Farmers never speak with an accent | Posh people never use any dialect | Cartoons must use only Standard English
test: What would happen if Kai wrote "we was" in his SATs grammar test? | He would lose the mark, as it asks for Standard English | He would get extra marks for using his own home dialect | Nothing, because tests accept every dialect | The test would be cancelled
test: A cousin from Newcastle says "Howay, man, we was robbed!" about a football match. ("Howay" means come on.) Is this wrong to say at the match? | No, it's natural dialect for talking with mates | Yes, it's always wrong to say "we was", even at a match | Yes, "howay" is not a real word | No, but only because it's about football
test: ★★★ Which is the only sentence with NO dialect forms in it? | I saw those books and I did my homework. | I seen those books and I did my homework. | I saw them books and I did my homework. | I saw those books and I done my homework.
test: A gran in Kerry says "Youse are grand." ("Grand" means fine.) Her grandson writes about her in a story. Should her words stay as she said them? | Yes, in speech marks, to show her voice | No, all dialect must be removed from stories | No, only Standard English may go in speech marks | Yes, but he must change "youse" to "you"
test: Your teacher, dry: "Innit is great at break time and not so great in a job application." What is she teaching? | Code-switching for the audience | That "innit" is a rude word | That job applications are written in slang | That break time has no rules
test: ★★★ "I didn't see nobody." Many languages, such as French and Spanish, use two negatives like this as standard. What does that show? | Double negatives are a real pattern, but not standard English | Double negatives are wrong in every language in the world | French and Spanish copied English dialects | They mean "I saw somebody" in every dialect
test: A Gen Alpha kid writes "this lesson is bussin no cap" in his English book. ("Bussin" means really good, "no cap" means for real.) What would Standard English be? | This lesson is really good, honestly. | This lesson are bussin, for real, no lie. | This lesson bussin, no cap, innit. | This lesson is no cap.
test: A girl from Texas says "I'm fixin' to go." ("Fixin' to" means about to.) Is that Standard English? | No, it's Southern US dialect for "I'm about to go" | Yes, it's the standard form in every English-speaking country | No, it means she is going to repair something | Yes, but only in writing
exam: Identify the sentence composed in Standard English. | We were late for school. | We was late for school. | Us was late for school. | We been late for school.
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between Standard English and regional dialects? | Standard English is one variety among many, conventionally used in formal writing | Standard English is the only variety possessing grammatical rules | Regional dialects are erroneous forms of Standard English | Regional dialects are not varieties of English
exam: Select the definition consistent with established usage of the term "code-switching". | The alteration of one's manner of speech according to audience and situation | The encryption of messages by means of a cipher | The translation of English into a foreign language | The replacement of letters by numerals
exam: Ascertain the Standard English equivalent of "I done it". | I did it | I have did it | I doned it | I do it yesterday
